[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Smart Contract Upgradeability Patterns in Blockchain"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because smart contracts deployed on blockchains like Ethereum are immutable by default, developers cannot modify their logic once deployed. However, business requirements change, and bugs are discovered. Upgradeability patterns solve this challenge by separating contract state from contract logic, allowing developers to deploy new logic while preserving existing data and addresses."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Proxy Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most widely used upgradeability pattern is the Proxy Pattern. It relies on two main contracts: a Proxy contract and a Logic (or Implementation) contract. Users interact exclusively with the Proxy contract, which holds the state (storage) and ether. When a user sends a transaction to the Proxy, the Proxy uses low-level assembly to delegate the call to the Implementation contract using delegatecall."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"contract Proxy {
    address public implementation;
    address public admin;

    constructor(address _implementation) {
        implementation = _implementation;
        admin = msg.sender;
    }

    function upgrade(address _newImplementation) external {
        require(msg.sender == admin, "Unauthorized");
        implementation = _newImplementation;
    }

    fallback() external payable {
        address impl = implementation;
        assembly {
            calldatacopy(0, 0, calldatasize())
            let result := delegatecall(gas(), impl, 0, calldatasize(), 0, 0)
            returndatacopy(0, 0, returndatasize())
            switch result
            case 0 { revert(0, returndatasize()) }
            default { return(0, returndatasize()) }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Storage Collision Risks"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A critical hazard in proxy architectures is storage collision. Because the Proxy contract holds the actual storage slots, the layout of state variables in both the Proxy and Implementation contracts must be meticulously managed. If an implementation contract defines a new state variable in a slot already occupied by the proxy administrative variables, corruption occurs."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never change the order or type of existing state variables in upgraded contracts."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always append new variables at the very end of the contract inheritance tree."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use structured storage patterns like EIP-1967 to store implementation addresses in pseudo-random storage slots."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Initialization instead of Constructors"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Constructors execute code during deployment and store state in the deployed contract's bytecode, which does not apply to proxy implementations because logic contracts are deployed independently. Therefore, upgradeable contracts use an initializer function instead of a constructor, protected by a boolean flag to ensure it runs only once."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"contract V1Implementation {
    address public owner;
    uint256 public value;
    bool private initialized;

    function initialize(address _owner) external {
        require(!initialized, "Already initialized");
        owner = _owner;
        initialized = true;
    }

    function setValue(uint256 _value) external {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Not owner");
        value = _value;
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Smart contract upgradeability introduces immense flexibility, but it comes at the cost of increased complexity and trust assumptions. Users must trust that the administrators holding upgrade keys will act maliciously. Utilizing battle-tested frameworks like OpenZeppelin Upgrades Plugins helps mitigate these risks by automating safety checks for storage layouts and initializers."}]
